SEC. 10101. RE-EVALUATION OF THRIFTY FOOD PLAN.

This section redefines the "thrifty food plan" used for SNAP allotments, basing it on the 2021 USDA report and freezing its market baskets until at least October 1, 2027. It sets fixed household size adjustment ratios (e.g., 1-person at 30%, 4-person at 100%, 8-person at 180% of the 4-person allotment). The Secretary may only adjust the plan's cost for inflation (CPI-U) annually and for geographic differences (Hawaii, Alaska, Guam, Virgin Islands). Any future re-evaluation of the market baskets must be cost-neutral, meaning it cannot increase the plan's cost.

SEC. 10102. MODIFICATIONS TO SNAP WORK REQUIREMENTS FOR ABLE-BODIED ADULTS.

This section expands exemptions from SNAP work requirements for able-bodied adults without dependents (ABAWDs) to include parents/caretakers of children under 14, pregnant women, and certain Native Americans. It also standardizes the unemployment rate waiver threshold for noncontiguous states (Alaska, Hawaii) at 1.5 times the national average. The Secretary may grant a temporary exemption to these states from work requirements until December 31, 2028, if they demonstrate a good faith effort toward compliance, subject to quarterly reporting. Guam and the Virgin Islands are excluded from this noncontiguous state exemption.

SEC. 10103. AVAILABILITY OF STANDARD UTILITY ALLOWANCES BASED ON RECEIPT OF ENERGY ASSISTANCE.

This section restricts the use of standard utility allowances (SUA) in SNAP. Specifically, it limits the availability of the SUA to households with an elderly or disabled member when they receive energy assistance. For households without an elderly or disabled member, third-party energy assistance payments will be counted as income, potentially reducing their SNAP benefits. This change makes it harder for non-elderly/disabled households to claim the SUA deduction.

SEC. 10104. RESTRICTIONS ON INTERNET EXPENSES.

This section amends the SNAP excess shelter expense deduction rules to explicitly prohibit any service fees associated with internet connection from being used in computing the deduction. This means households can no longer deduct internet costs as part of their shelter expenses when calculating SNAP eligibility and benefit amounts.

SEC. 10105. MATCHING FUNDS REQUIREMENTS.

This section introduces a new State cost-sharing requirement for SNAP benefits based on payment error rates, beginning in fiscal year 2028. States with error rates below 6% will have the federal government cover 100% of benefit costs, while states with higher error rates will see the federal share drop to 95%, 90%, or 85%, requiring the state to cover the difference. States can use their error rates from FY2025 or FY2026 for the first year, and the Secretary will use a three-year lag for subsequent years. Implementation may be delayed to FY2029 or FY2030 for states with very high error rates.

SEC. 10106. ADMINISTRATIVE COST SHARING.

This section reduces the federal government's share of SNAP administrative costs from 50% to 25% for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter. The 50% federal share remains in effect through fiscal year 2026. This change will require states to cover a larger portion of their SNAP program administration costs.

SEC. 10107. NATIONAL EDUCATION AND OBESITY PREVENTION GRANT PROGRAM.

This section limits the authorization for the National Education and Obesity Prevention Grant Program to fiscal years 2016 through 2025. The program, which provides grants for SNAP-Ed activities, will no longer be authorized for fiscal year 2026 and subsequent years, effectively ending its mandatory funding.

SEC. 10108. ALIEN SNAP ELIGIBILITY.

This section restricts SNAP eligibility for non-citizens to only U.S. citizens/nationals, lawful permanent residents (excluding temporary visitors, tourists, diplomats, and students), Cuban/Haitian entrants, and individuals residing in the U.S. under a Compact of Free Association. The income and resources of ineligible non-citizens in a household will still be counted when determining the eligibility and benefit amount for the remaining eligible household members.

SEC. 10201. RESCISSION OF AMOUNTS FOR FORESTRY.

This section rescinds unobligated balances of funds appropriated for forestry programs by the Inflation Reduction Act (Public Law 117-169). It specifically cancels funding from sections 23001(a)(3) and (4), 23002(a)(1) through (4), 23003(a)(2), and 23005 of that law. This action reduces available funding for these forestry initiatives.

SEC. 10301. EFFECTIVE REFERENCE PRICE; REFERENCE PRICE.

This section updates the reference prices used for Price Loss Coverage (PLC) payments, effective with the 2025 crop year. It sets new, higher reference prices for covered commodities (e.g., wheat at $6.35/bushel, corn at $4.10/bushel, soybeans at $10.00/bushel). Starting in 2031, these prices will increase by 0.5% annually, but cannot exceed 113% of the initial 2025 levels. The effective reference price used for payment calculations is also increased from 85% to 88% of the reference price.

SEC. 10302. BASE ACRES.

This section creates a new allocation of up to 30,000,000 additional base acres for farms, effective with the 2026 crop year. Eligibility is based on a farm's 5-year average (2019-2023) of planted and prevented-planted acreage of covered commodities and certain noncovered commodities, compared to its existing base acres. The Secretary will allocate these acres proportionally based on planting history, and new payment yields will be established for the new base acres. Farms with no recent plantings of covered commodities are ineligible.

SEC. 10303. PRODUCER ELECTION.

This section extends the deadline for producers to make a one-time election between Price Loss Coverage (PLC) and Agriculture Risk Coverage (ARC) for the 2026 crop year. It also allows producers to elect the same coverage for the 2027-2031 crop years as they had for the 2025 crop year. For the 2025 crop year, the Secretary is directed to pay producers the higher of PLC or ARC county coverage payments on a commodity-by-commodity basis. It also removes a restriction that prevented producers who elected ARC from buying Supplemental Coverage Option (SCO) crop insurance.

SEC. 10304. PRICE LOSS COVERAGE.

This section extends the PLC program through the 2031 crop year. It updates the payment calculation period from 2025 to 2031 and changes the historical payment yield calculation base from the 2012-2016 period to the 2017-2021 period. It also corrects a statutory reference for the PLC payment rate formula.

SEC. 10305. AGRICULTURE RISK COVERAGE.

This section extends the ARC program through the 2031 crop year. It changes the ARC benchmark revenue calculation to use 90% of the benchmark for the 2025-2031 crop years (down from 100% for 2014-2024). The ARC payment trigger (the "ARC guarantee") is increased from 86% to 88% of the benchmark revenue for the 2025-2031 crop years. This makes ARC payments slightly less generous but more likely to trigger.

SEC. 10306. EQUITABLE TREATMENT OF CERTAIN ENTITIES.

This section expands the definition of "qualified pass-through entity" for farm program payment eligibility to include partnerships, S corporations, and limited liability companies (LLCs) that don't elect to be taxed as corporations. It applies this new definition to rules on payment attribution, actively engaged in farming requirements, and joint and several liability. This change allows more business structures to be treated similarly for payment limitation purposes.

SEC. 10307. PAYMENT LIMITATIONS.

This section increases the annual payment limitation for price loss coverage and agriculture risk coverage payments from $125,000 to $155,000 per person or legal entity. It also requires the Secretary to adjust these payment limits annually for inflation based on the Consumer Price Index, starting with the 2025 crop year.

SEC. 10308. ADJUSTED GROSS INCOME LIMITATION.

This section creates an exception to the adjusted gross income (AGI) limitation for certain farm payments. The exception applies to payments for disaster assistance, cotton transition payments, and certain other benefits if at least 75% of the person or entity's average gross income comes from farming, ranching, or silviculture activities. This allows farmers with high off-farm income to still receive these specific payments if they are primarily engaged in farming.

SEC. 10309. MARKETING LOANS.

This section extends the nonrecourse marketing assistance loan program through the 2031 crop year. It sets new, lower loan rates for the 2026-2031 crop years for commodities like wheat ($3.72/bu), corn ($2.42/bu), and soybeans ($6.82/bu). It also increases the loan rate for peanuts to $390/ton and provides for cotton storage payments for the 2026-2031 crop years. The loan deficiency payment program is also extended through 2031.

SEC. 10310. REPAYMENT OF MARKETING LOANS.

This section modifies the repayment rules for marketing assistance loans. For upland cotton, it provides a refund to producers if the world market price drops within 30 days after loan repayment. For other commodities, it allows repayment at the lesser of the loan rate or the prevailing world market price. It also establishes a specific formula for determining the world market price for extra long staple cotton and extends the special competitive provisions for that commodity through 2032.

SEC. 10311. ECONOMIC ADJUSTMENT ASSISTANCE FOR TEXTILE MILLS.

This section increases the value of economic adjustment assistance provided to textile mills from 3 cents per pound to 5 cents per pound, effective August 1, 2025. The 3-cent rate remains in effect for the period from August 1, 2013, through July 31, 2025.

SEC. 10312. SUGAR PROGRAM UPDATES.

This section updates the sugar program. It sets the loan rate for raw cane sugar at 24.00 cents per pound for the 2025-2031 crop years and sets the beet sugar loan rate at 136.55% of the cane sugar rate. It also establishes minimum storage rates for forfeited sugar and modernizes beet sugar allotment allocation rules. The Secretary is directed to study and potentially implement new terms and conditions for refined sugar imports.

SEC. 10313. DAIRY POLICY UPDATES.

This section updates the Dairy Margin Coverage (DMC) program. It changes the production history calculation to be based on the highest annual milk marketings from 2021, 2022, or 2023. It increases the tier I coverage limit from 5 million to 6 million pounds of milk production. It also extends the DMC program through 2031 and adjusts the premium discount schedule for the 2026-2031 period.

SEC. 10314. IMPLEMENTATION.

This section provides $50 million in mandatory funding from the Commodity Credit Corporation to implement the commodity title changes. The funds are allocated for specific purposes, including $5 million for base acre and payment yield work, $3 million for producer election outreach, $9 million for dairy cost surveys, and $1 million for a sugar import study. The funds remain available until expended.

SEC. 10401. SUPPLEMENTAL AGRICULTURAL DISASTER ASSISTANCE.

This section modifies several disaster assistance programs. It increases livestock indemnity payments for predation losses to 100% of market value and for adverse weather/disease losses to 75%. It adds a new payment for unborn livestock losses and expands the Livestock Forage Disaster Program to provide two monthly payments for drought conditions. It also creates a new payment for farm-raised fish losses due to bird depredation and lowers the tree assistance program damage threshold to "normal mortality."

SEC. 10501. BEGINNING FARMER AND RANCHER BENEFIT.

This section expands the definition of a "beginning farmer or rancher" from having 5 to 10 years of farming experience. It also provides additional premium support for beginning farmers and ranchers: 5 percentage points for the first two years, 3 points for the third year, and 1 point for the fourth year of participation in a crop insurance policy.

SEC. 10502. AREA-BASED CROP INSURANCE COVERAGE AND AFFORDABILITY.

This section increases the maximum coverage level for area-based crop insurance plans from 85% to 95%. It also increases the premium subsidy for area-based plans from 65% to 80%. This makes area-based coverage more affordable and allows producers to insure a higher percentage of their expected yield or revenue.

SEC. 10503. ADMINISTRATIVE AND OPERATING EXPENSE ADJUSTMENTS.

This section increases administrative and operating (A&O) expense reimbursements for crop insurance providers. It adds a new payment equal to 6% of net book premium for eligible contracts in states with high loss ratios. It also sets a minimum A&O reimbursement rate of 17% of premium for specialty crops and requires an inflation adjustment to A&O reimbursements starting in 2026.

SEC. 10504. PREMIUM SUPPORT.

This section increases the federal premium subsidy rates for crop insurance at various coverage levels. For example, the subsidy for 70% coverage increases from 64% to 69%, and for 80% coverage from 48% to 51%. This makes crop insurance more affordable for producers across all coverage levels.

SEC. 10505. PROGRAM COMPLIANCE AND INTEGRITY.

This section increases the annual funding for the crop insurance program's compliance and integrity efforts from $4 million to $6 million, starting in fiscal year 2026. The $4 million level remains in effect for fiscal years 2009 through 2025.

SEC. 10506. REVIEWS, COMPLIANCE, AND INTEGRITY.

This section increases the annual funding for the crop insurance program's reviews, compliance, and integrity activities from $4 million to $10 million, starting in fiscal year 2026. The $4 million level remains in effect for fiscal years 2014 through 2025.

SEC. 10507. POULTRY INSURANCE PILOT PROGRAM.

This section directs the Federal Crop Insurance Corporation to establish a pilot program offering index-based insurance to contract poultry growers (broilers and laying hens) for extreme weather-related risks that increase utility costs. The program will be conducted in top poultry-producing states and must be approved by the Board within two years of enactment.

SEC. 10601. CONSERVATION.

This section increases mandatory funding for several conservation programs for fiscal years 2026-2031. It provides increased funding for the Environmental Quality Incentives Program (EQIP), the Conservation Stewardship Program (CSP), and the Agricultural Conservation Easement Program (ACEP). It also increases funding for the Regional Conservation Partnership Program (RCPP) and the Watershed Protection and Flood Prevention Program, and provides new funding for the Voluntary Public Access and Habitat Incentive Program and the Feral Swine Eradication and Control Pilot Program.

SEC. 10602. SUPPLEMENTAL AGRICULTURAL TRADE PROMOTION PROGRAM.

This section establishes a new program to encourage the development and expansion of commercial export markets for U.S. agricultural commodities. It provides $285 million in mandatory funding from the Commodity Credit Corporation for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter.

SEC. 10603. NUTRITION.

This section extends the authorization for the Emergency Food Assistance Program (TEFAP) through fiscal year 2031. The previous authorization was set to expire in 2024.

SEC. 10604. RESEARCH.

This section provides new mandatory funding for several agricultural research programs. It extends the Urban, Indoor, and Other Emerging Agricultural Production Research Initiative through 2031 and provides $37 million for the Foundation for Food and Agriculture Research. It also provides $60 million for scholarships at 1890 Institutions, $8 million for the Assistive Technology Program for Farmers with Disabilities, and $175 million for the Specialty Crop Research Initiative in FY2026.

SEC. 10605. ENERGY.

This section extends the authorization for the Biorefinery, Renewable Chemical, and Biobased Product Manufacturing Assistance Program through fiscal year 2031. The previous authorization was set to expire in 2024.

SEC. 10606. HORTICULTURE.

This section increases mandatory funding for several horticulture programs. It increases funding for the Plant Pest and Disease Management and Disaster Prevention Program to $90 million in FY2026 and for Specialty Crop Block Grants to $100 million in FY2026. It also provides new funding for the Organic Production and Market Data Initiative and the National Organic Certification Cost-share Program.

SEC. 10607. MISCELLANEOUS.

This section provides new mandatory funding for various agricultural programs. It increases funding for the Animal Disease Prevention and Management Program to $233 million per year for FY2026-2030. It also provides funding for the Sheep Production and Marketing Grant Program, the Pima Agriculture Cotton Trust Fund, the Agriculture Wool Apparel Manufacturers Trust Fund, and the Emergency Citrus Disease Research and Development Trust Fund. [end of text]


